    </div>
  </div>
</div>`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Scrollspy · Bootstrap HTML"
  description="Native CSS scrollspy using scroll-target-group and :target-current, styled with Bootstrap 5.3.8."
>
  <h1>Scrollspy</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Highlight the in-page link that matches the section in view. CSS
    <code>scroll-target-group</code> and <code>:target-current</code>
    replace the ScrollSpy plugin — no JavaScript.
  </p>

  <DocsMeta id="scrollspy" at="start" />

  <div class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
    <strong>Experimental technology.</strong>
    <code>scroll-target-group</code> and <code>:target-current</code>
    are experimental CSS (Chrome 140+, not native in Firefox or Safari).
    This site loads an IntersectionObserver polyfill only when
    <code>CSS.supports('scroll-target-group: auto')</code> is false.
    Check the
    <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Properties/scroll-target-group">
      MDN page for <code>scroll-target-group</code>
    </a>
    and the
    <a href={sitePath("/polyfills#scroll-target-group")}>polyfill notes</a>
    before using this in production.
  </div>

  <h2 class="h4">How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Put in-page links (<code>href=&quot;#id&quot;</code>) in a nav,
      list group, or any group of anchors. Mark that group with
      <code>data-polyfill-scrollspy</code>. That sets
      <code>scroll-target-group: auto</code> for native CSS and is
      what the polyfill looks for (replaces
      <code>data-bs-spy=&quot;scroll&quot;</code> /
      <code>data-bs-target</code>). An optional selector value points
      at the scroller; otherwise the overflow ancestor of the targets
      is used.
    </li>
    <li>
      Give each section an <code>id</code> that matches a link. The
      scrollable box can be the page or an element with a height and
      <code>overflow: auto</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>:target-current</code> styles the marker whose target is in
      view. It supplements <code>.active</code> with the same look, so
      either class or the CSS scroll marker can highlight a link.
    </li>
    <li>
      A parent <code>.nav-link</code> or dropdown toggle stays
      highlighted with <code>:has(:target-current)</code> when a nested
      marker is current.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>scroll-behavior: smooth</code> on the scroller replaces
      <code>data-bs-smooth-scroll</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Example
    title="Navbar"
    lead="Scroll the area below the navbar and watch the current link change. Open the dropdown to see its items highlighted too."
    code={examples.scrollspy}
  >
    <div slot="alert" class="alert alert-warning" role="alert">
      Closed <code>[popover]</code> menus use
      <code>display: none</code>, so <code>.dropdown-item</code>
      fragment links are not scroll markers and the toggle never sees
      <code>:target-current</code>. Copy each <code>href</code> into a
      <code>.scroll-marker-alias</code> next to the toggle
      (<code>aria-hidden=&quot;true&quot;</code>,
      <code>tabindex=&quot;-1&quot;</code>) so the browser can still
      pick a current marker and
      <code>:has(:target-current)</code> can style the dropdown as
      active. While the menu is open, CSS hides the alias so the
      visible item becomes <code>:target-current</code> instead.
    </div>
  </Example>

  <Example
    title="Nested nav"
    lead="Scrollspy also works with nested .navs. If a nested link is current, its parent nav-link is highlighted as well."
    code={nestedCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="List group"
    lead="Scrollspy also works with .list-groups. Scroll the area next to the list group and watch the current item change."
    code={listGroupCode}
  />

  <Example
    title="Simple anchors"
    lead="Scrollspy is not limited to nav components and list groups. Any fragment link in a data-polyfill-scrollspy group works."
    code={simpleCode}
  />

  <DocsMeta id="scrollspy" at="end" />
</BaseLayout>
